## THE DECK INTRINSIC: why no rung that lexes would run, and it was ours

The first rung died in 5 s and the answer took most of the morning and two
wrong turns. Written out because the mechanism is five links long and every
one of them is correct on its own.

- **The fault.** `lex` and later `desugar` both page-faulted at
  `tokenize-collect+546` -- same function, same offset, stack
  `opening -> tokenize -> tokenize-collect`, resolved against each subject's
  own `.cdx.map`. `!EXC=0e`, CR2 a wild `0x2_0000000f`.
- **What changed.** `Syntax/Lexer.codex` gained `lex-state-below`: 0 uses at
  U53, 4 at U54. `tokenize-collect` now rewinds the bivy after every token and
  is correct only if `make-token`'s record was really moved to the deck first.
- **Why it was not.** `deck-record` is the IDENTITY in source -- in
  `Core/PhaseAllocator.codex` byte-for-byte as in our stub. The real behaviour
  is a call-site transform, `emit-deck-record-wrapper`, gated on
  `deck-record-intrinsic`, and the gate asks whether `init-phase-allocator` is
  among the IR DEFS (`X86_64Chapter.codex:1264`).
- **Why the def was missing.** Our harness openings never CALLED it. The real
  driver's `compile-lex` opens with `init-phase-allocator`, then `build
  lex-deck-height`, then passes a ceiling into `tokenize-into`. Ours called
  bare `tokenize`. With no caller the def is unreachable and
  `ir-prune-unreachable-roots` (`opening.codex:1359`, the seed's ordinary CDX
  path) removed it -- so the gate read "no phase allocator" and every
  `deck-record` compiled to `mov rax,rdi ; ret`.
- **CARRYING THE CHAPTER IS NOT ENOUGH, and that is the sharp part.** `desugar`
  bundles `Core/PhaseAllocator.codex` and faulted identically. A bundle can
  satisfy every cite, carry the right chapter, and still be compiled without
  the mechanism, because the gate's input is something dead-code elimination
  can take away.

**THE LADDER ALREADY KNEW, IN FOUR PLACES.** `lower`, `ir_to_codex`,
`ir_to_wire` and `ir_to_codex_roundtrip` call `DECK_PROLOGUE`, and the comment
above each says why in the words this incident would have used: "Without it
deck-record compiles to `mov rax,rdi ; ret` and the deck discipline is off for
the whole unit, **which is the condition that faulted clamp**." Four bytes,
which is exactly what `deck-record` measured as in both faulting maps. The fix
reached four harnesses of twelve and stopped. This is the second time in one
day -- `emit_harness.py` records `ir-emit-roots` drifting from six to four in
BOTH hosted harnesses at once, where "being wrong together looks exactly like
being right."

**Confirmed by one variable before being applied.** desugar, prologue only,
bundle untouched: 923-byte fault dump -> 6,041-byte truth, `lex-tokens 5727`,
`lex-errors 0`. Presence check: `init-phase-allocator` ABSENT from the old
symbol map, at `0x0010B8FE` in the new one, and the subject grew 438,050 ->
525,673 bytes, which is the `__deck-enter`/`__deck-exit` pairs the gate flip
emits.

**Scope, deliberately narrow.** Five rungs call `tokenize` -- lex, parse,
desugar, scope, check. `ir_to_x86`, `lir_to_x86` and `passes_to_x86` consume IR
and never lex, so they are untouched. lex and parse also gained the chapter and
`BootPaintStubs`; `LexStubs` then gave up `deck-record` and `deck-short-of`,
and parse dropped `LexStubs` entirely once CDX3006 caught the duplicate
`copy-sx-diag` against the real one in `SyntaxNodes`.

**Two wrong turns, recorded because the pattern outlives the bug.** First:
"our stub is wrong" -- killed by desugar, which carries the real chapter and
crashed identically. Second: "the guest never started" -- it started, faulted
in a fraction of a second, and sat with its dump in a buffer nobody was
reading; zero CPU meant crashed immediately, not never ran. Both readings ran
in the direction of a theory already formed. The essay is
`notes/the-harness-that-skipped-the-setup.md`.

## WHERE THIS STOPS, 2026-09-02

**Five rungs bank clean at U54: lex, parse, desugar, scope, check.** Timings
all slightly under U53 (lex 15s/17s, parse 45/56, desugar 57/64, scope 68/71,
check 126/129). The sixth, `lower`, faults and the run stops there.

**`lower` faults `!EXC=0d` at `__str_concat+72`, and it is NOT the deck
ceiling.** Two runs either side of that fix are byte-identical in outcome --
same 65-line file, same 1,660 bytes, R13 identical to the bit
(`f883480824448b4c`, which decodes as x86 instruction bytes: a register holding
code), only heap addresses moving. The commit that added the ceiling is right
about zero-ceilings disabling the guard and WRONG that it fixed this.

**THE FAULT GATE EARNED ITS KEEP TWICE**, and the second was the harder case:
`lower.truth` was 1,660 bytes with 46 lines of correct output and the dump
appended, which reads like a truth. Stamping refused, the five banked rungs
stand, and the run stopped rather than building on a bad foundation.

## LOWER IS GREEN, AND IT WAS NEITHER CANDIDATE (2026-09-02, later)

**`check-chapter` was being called BARE where the driver wraps it in
`deck-record`.** One-line fix in `check_call()`, and `lower` banks an 83-line
truth carrying the three bindings whose Text was corrupt -- `tb fib fn`,
`tb double fn`, `tb opening eff` -- and the whole IR tree after them.

**COMPILER-38 is FALSIFIED, and cleanly.** `lower` with `rename = False`
faulted with every register byte-identical to the original: RIP, RBX, R13,
R10, RDI, RSI, CR2, R15 and the entire stack window. The one difference in the
whole 65-line file is `F[00]` moving four bytes, `0x211c90` -> `0x211c8c`,
which is the code address shifting because the harness source changed by one
word. Even the heap addresses held.

**The scale candidate is closed by arithmetic and never needed a run.** The
four raw floors total 1,232 MB against derived-77's 954 MB, in a 3,072 MB guest
whose stack sits at 3,072 MB and whose frontier at the fault was 1,247 MB.
There is 1.8 GB of slack either way. Correcting the scale is still right for
fidelity; it was never a cause.

**WHAT THE READING SAID THAT THE DUMP DID NOT.** `emit-let`
(`Emit/X86_64.codex:2274`) emits the value into a local and then the body, so
Codex `let` is STRICT and a harness computes every binding before it prints a
line. Forty-six lines of output therefore means lowering FINISHED; the fault is
in the print. The print after the last good line is `show-bindings`, whose text
is `"tb " & b.name & " " & ...`, and the registers agree independently -- `RDI`
a fresh heap text 15 MB above the top reservation, `RSI` at 2.16 MB in static
data, which is the `" "` literal. The faulting call is the SECOND `&`, reading
a CHECK-phase artifact, one phase behind where this log had placed it.

**The mechanism, from the compiler's own source.** `check-chapter` issues a
`__deck-exit` before its per-definition walk and a `__deck-enter` after it
(`TypeChecker.codex:2388,2393`) -- the only hand-written pair outside the
emitter -- so the walk runs outside the phase extent and `deck-short-of` reads
a live cell. `BuildSettings.codex:154` states the requirement outright. The
extent it exits is the CALLER'S, and the caller upstream ships is
`opening.codex:631`. Called bare, the nesting counter runs to -1, where
`emit-deck-enter-builtin`'s zero-crossing test makes every `deck-record` in the
walk a no-op: results land on the bivy while `check-batch` reclaims to
`cb-bivy-mark` as though they had not. `check` prints `cr.types` on the next
line and never notices. `lower` allocates the resolved tables, a 328 MB
reservation and the whole of lowering first, then reads a Text whose length
word is someone else's data.

**Both candidates were about LOWER and the bug was in CHECK.** The rung that
fails is not the rung that is wrong when the failing rung is the first one to
read the earlier one's result LATE.

**THE SANDBOX WAS RETIRED, not kept.** `20260902T121126Z-u54-rebank` is gone,
worktrees and all, and the five working truths went with it -- BOX After-4's
default, and the KEEP written for it did not earn the exception. What survives
is `findings/deckfaults/`: both fault dumps, the symbol map without which the
