  with no change on our side -- "the independent arm we do not have, and worth
  more than our three." `recursive-eq.no-cross` is deleted in u54. This falls
  out of the corpus run for free.
- **PR 103 left plugs row 2.02 OPEN**, and the open half is ours to know
  about: our claim that a payload-carrying constructor is never a duplicate is
  wrong. `emit-zig-match-arm` emits a BARE prong when the binders are all
  unused (`:2155` one sub, `:2158` several), so two such arms still collide and
  still refuse at zig. `zig-pat-switch-value` only answers for
  `list-length subs == 0`.
- **PR 101's house rule, for every PR after this one:** column-2 prose is
  reserved for external formats, magic numbers and performance
  characteristics. Roughly half our added lines were prose explaining their own
  code, and they dropped it.

## THE REBANK REACHED 8 OF 12, AND RUNG 9 IS A U54 BUNDLE GAP (2026-09-02)

Sandbox `20260902T151248Z-u54-rebank-deckextent`, ladder `7eb5878`, KEPT --
eight recorded truths are in it and they are ~13 minutes of guests.

    lex 15s · parse 46s · desugar 57s · scope 70s · check 118s · lower 152s
    ir_to_codex 148s · ir_to_codex_roundtrip 148s          8/12, 760s in

`check` is green WITH the wrapper and its truth is intact, so the fix is
neutral for the phase that was already passing and decisive for the one that
was not. `lower.truth` is byte-identical to the isolated run that had
`check-chapter` wrapped but `lower-chapter` still bare -- so wrapping
lower-chapter moved where lowering allocates and not one byte of what it
produces. The check-chapter wrapper alone was the whole fix.

**Rung 9, `lir_to_x86`, fails to COMPILE and it is nothing to do with the
harness.** Five CDX3002 at codegen:

    hosted-win-load-addr   hosted-win-heap-base   hosted-win-cell-base
    hosted-cell-base       bare-metal-heap-base

U54 gave `X86_64State.codex` a Windows-hosted target selection --
`st-load-base`, `st-heap-base`, `st-base` each branch on `st-windows` -- and
the constants live on two OTHER pages of the same fourteen-page chapter. The
first four are NEW AT U54 in `Emit/X86_64Boot.codex`; `bare-metal-heap-base`
is in `Emit/X86_64Chapter.codex` at both Updates and State only started
reading it now. Measured by `git grep` across `u53-rebank` and `u54-rebank`.

`bundle_lir_to_x86.ps1` carries three of the fourteen pages on purpose -- it is
the small back-half slice, 489 KB, no front end at all -- and `bundle_ir_to_x86
.ps1` carries all fifteen. So the choice at rung 9 is:

  - **add the two pages** (3,684 + 1,292 lines) -- faithful, and the bundle
    roughly doubles; their own references may cascade and that is unmeasured;
  - **extend `LirStubs.codex`**, which already exists for exactly this and
    already carries "faithful copies from unbundled pages ... kept
    byte-for-byte with their originals" (tco-list-contains, emit-call-to,
    buf-write-i32). Five Integer constants is the smallest possible instance
    of what that file is already for.

**Not decided, and it is a real decision** -- the stub route is cheap and its
risk is a VALUE drifting silently at U55, which is the standing cost of every
stub in `ast/`. The page route has no drift risk and an unmeasured blast
radius. Nothing was run either way.

Rungs 10-12 are unreached: `ir_to_wire`, then `ir_to_x86` and `passes_to_x86`
-- and those last two are the ones that used `frontend_source`, so the U54
arity fix in `emit_harness.py` is still UNTESTED by a run.
